CREATE TABLE employees (
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT,
    salary DECIMAL(10,2),
    department VARCHAR(50),
    city VARCHAR(50)
);

1. Update a single column
UPDATE employees
SET salary = 50000
WHERE id = 1;

Changes only salary for employee id = 1.

2. Update multiple columns
UPDATE employees
SET salary = 55000,
    city = 'Mumbai'
WHERE id = 1;

3. Update all rows
If you don't use WHERE, every row will be updated.

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 50000;

⚠️ Be careful with this.

4. Update using an existing column
You can calculate a new value from the old value.

UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + 5000
WHERE id = 1;

For example, increase everyone's salary by 10%:

UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary * 1.10;

5. Update a string/text column
UPDATE employees
SET name = 'Rahul'
WHERE id = 1;

6. Update a numeric column
UPDATE employees
SET age = 30
WHERE id = 1;

7. Update a date column
If you have a date column:

UPDATE employees
SET joining_date = '2026-08-17'
WHERE id = 1;

8. Update using multiple conditions
UPDATE employees
SET salary = 60000
WHERE department = 'IT'
AND city = 'Mumbai';

9. Update using OR
UPDATE employees
SET salary = 60000
WHERE department = 'IT'
OR department = 'HR';

10. Update using comparison operators
UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + 5000
WHERE salary < 50000;

You can use:

=       -- equal
<>      -- not equal
>       -- greater than
<       -- less than
>=      -- greater than or equal
<=      -- less than or equal

Example:

UPDATE employees
SET department = 'Senior IT'
WHERE salary >= 80000;

11. Update using IN
UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + 3000
WHERE department IN ('IT', 'HR', 'Sales');

12. Update using BETWEEN
UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + 2000
WHERE salary BETWEEN 40000 AND 60000;

13. Update using LIKE
UPDATE employees
SET department = 'IT'
WHERE name LIKE 'Rah%';

This updates employees whose names start with Rah.

14. Update using NULL
Set a column to NULL:

UPDATE employees
SET city = NULL
WHERE id = 1;

Find rows where a column is NULL:

UPDATE employees
SET city = 'Mumbai'
WHERE city IS NULL;

15. Update using CASE
You can give different values to different rows.

UPDATE employees
SET salary = CASE
    WHEN department = 'IT' THEN salary + 10000
    WHEN department = 'HR' THEN salary + 5000
    ELSE salary
END;

16. Update with a function
For example, convert a name to uppercase:

UPDATE employees
SET name = UPPER(name)
WHERE id = 1;

Convert to lowercase:

UPDATE employees
SET name = LOWER(name)
WHERE id = 1;

17. Update part of a string
Using CONCAT():

UPDATE employees
SET name = CONCAT(name, ' Kumar')
WHERE id = 1;

