WHERE Clause in MySQL
What does WHERE do?
The WHERE clause is used to filter rows from a table.
GROUP BY in MySQL
What does GROUP BY do?
GROUP BY puts rows with the same value into groups.

It is mainly used with aggregate functions such as:

COUNT()
SUM()
AVG()
MIN()
MAX()

Basic syntax
SELECT column, AGGREGATE_FUNCTION(column)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column;

SELECT department, gender, COUNT(*)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department, gender;

HAVING Clause in MySQL
What is HAVING?
HAVING is used to filter groups created by GROUP BY.
Use HAVING when you want to filter based on an aggregate function, such as:

COUNT()
SUM()
AVG()
MIN()
MAX()

Basic syntax
SELECT column, AGGREGATE_FUNCTION(column)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column
HAVING condition;
SELECT department, MAX(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department
HAVING MAX(salary) > 100000;

Multi-row functions perform calculations on multiple rows and return one result for the group/set of rows.

They are also commonly called aggregate functions.

Main Multi-Row Functions
COUNT()
SUM()
AVG()
MIN()
MAX()

