What is a Subquery in MySQL?
A subquery is simply a query inside another query.
ALL and ANY Operators in MySQL
ALL and ANY are used with a subquery to compare one value against multiple values returned by the subquery.
employees
---------------------------
name   | salary | dept_id
Rahul  | 40000  | 10
Amit   | 60000  | 20
Priya  | 80000  | 20
John   | 50000  | 10

1. ANY
ANY means:

The condition must be true for at least ONE value returned by the subquery.

For example:

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary > ANY (
    SELECT salary
    FROM employees
    WHERE dept_id = 10
);

The subquery returns:

40000
50000

Now MySQL checks:

salary > 40000
OR
salary > 50000

If at least one is true → the employee is selected.

So:

60000 > 40000  ✅
60000 > 50000  ✅

80000 > 40000  ✅
80000 > 50000  ✅

Both Amit and Priya qualify.

Easy meaning:
ANY = at least one

2. ALL
ALL means:

The condition must be true for EVERY value returned by the subquery.

Example:

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary > ALL (
    SELECT salary
    FROM employees
    WHERE dept_id = 10
);

Subquery:

40000
50000

Now the salary must be:

salary > 40000
AND
salary > 50000

Therefore the salary must be greater than both values.

So:

60000 → ✅
80000 → ✅
50000 → ❌
40000 → ❌

Easy meaning:
ALL = every one

ANY vs ALL
This is the most important part:

Operator	Meaning
> ANY	Greater than at least one
> ALL	Greater than every value
< ANY	Less than at least one
< ALL	Less than every value
= ANY	Equal to at least one
= ALL	Equal to every value

Think about numbers
Suppose the subquery gives:

10
20
30

> ANY
WHERE x > ANY (10, 20, 30)

x only needs to be greater than one value.

For example:

x = 15

15 > 10 ✅
15 > 20 ❌
15 > 30 ❌

At least one is true → YES.

> ALL
WHERE x > ALL (10, 20, 30)

x must be greater than every value.

For example:

x = 35

35 > 10 ✅
35 > 20 ✅
35 > 30 ✅

All are true → YES.

Very useful shortcut ⭐
For positive comparisons:

x > ANY (10,20,30)

is effectively:

x > MIN(10,20,30)

So:

x > ANY → x > smallest value

And:

x > ALL → x > largest value

Similarly:

x < ANY → x < largest value

x < ALL → x < smallest value

ANY with a real subquery
Find employees whose salary is greater than at least one employee in department 10:

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary > ANY (
    SELECT salary
    FROM employees
    WHERE dept_id = 10
);

A JOIN in SQL is used to combine rows from two or more tables based on a related column or condition between those tables.


A LEFT JOIN returns all records from the left table and the matching records from the right table. If there is no matching record in the right table, it returns NULL for the right table’s columns.
